  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 1,014 feet, <strong>Mount Pisgah</strong> is a summit in Ohio. The Mount Pisgah Trail covers 2.6 miles with about 397 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The climbing is spread fairly evenly from start to finish rather than stacked into one steep section. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0, gaining roughly 700 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 712 feet, the route tops out near 1,109 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=39.22516,-82.91849\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">39.22516, -82.91849</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 175 miles southwest of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Spring through fall is the main season. Conditions change fast in the shoulder months \u2014 check a point forecast for the summit, not the nearest town, before you commit.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 1.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Chimney Rocks, Twin Knobs and Mount Logan \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
